// expect-exit: 0
//
// THE CONSUMER. The first program in this repository that is not a probe, and
// the only one whose subject is the library's PUBLIC SURFACE rather than a
// language shape. It compiles against an almost-empty surface on purpose: it
// keeps compiling as the surface grows, and the day it stops, the surface
// changed in a way a consumer can see (`0.0.1.md` §3 step 2).
//
// WHAT IT PROVES, and each of these is a separate failure mode:
//
//   1. `src/lib.npk` can be imported by a program by RELATIVE PATH.
//   2. The umbrella's `pub use` actually re-exports. A plain `use` does not,
//      and the difference is invisible in `lib.npk` — it surfaces here, as
//      `cannot find ERegexPattern in this scope`. See RX-113.
//   3. IMPORTING `nregex` COSTS A PROGRAM'S `failsafe` EXACTLY ONE ARM.
//      `(ERegexPattern)` below is the whole price of the library (RX-060,
//      `SAFETY.md` S-8), and it is worth seeing in the first test that exists,
//      because REACH-002 makes every additional `error:` a compile-time break
//      in every consuming program — a second identity is a MAJOR version.
//   4. The program LINKS AND RUNS. `npkc` exiting 0 does not mean a program is
//      well-formed (registry O-N11, the compiler's DEF-5), so this file is
//      judged by its exit code and never by `npkc`'s.
//
// THE IMPORT PATH IS RELATIVE BECAUSE IT HAS TO BE — `O-G3`. `[dependencies]`
// is parsed and the loader's dependency-root list is created empty and never
// populated, so the package form `use "nregex/lib.npk".*;` resolves against an
// empty set (`BUILD.md` §1, rule B-15; the registry files it as `O-N2`). The
// day dependency roots work, this line and its siblings are what change, and
// they are greppable by this comment.
mod:import;

use "../../src/lib.npk".*;

// Nothing is called. The library has no callable surface yet, and inventing
// one here to have something to call would test this file rather than the
// import.
func:main = int32(int32:argc, cstring[]:argv) {
    // `_~argv` would mark the parameter DISCARDED and reading it would be
    // `NITPICK-TYPE-007`; `discard(x)` marks it USED. The parentheses are
    // mandatory.
    discard(argc);
    discard(argv);
    exit 0i32;
};

// THE SYSTEM ARMS PLUS EXACTLY ONE OF OURS.
//
// `(*)` and the named arms discharge two DIFFERENT obligations and neither
// covers the other: omitting the wildcard is `NITPICK-PICK-003`
// (exhaustiveness — `Error` has more values than a `pick` can list), and
// omitting a reachable identity is `NITPICK-REACH-002`, which says so in as
// many words: "`(*)` counts for nothing here".
//
// `(ERegexPattern)` is named although nothing in this program can raise it —
// there is no callable surface yet. That is accepted: a `failsafe` may name an
// error that cannot reach it, and there is no unreachable-arm diagnostic
// (measured, 0.0.1). It is named deliberately, so that this file states the
// library's whole cost from the first day and a second identity appearing
// would have to be added here by hand.
func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (ERegexPattern)  { exit 60i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
